+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql cannot be applied to production
+
+`users.email` must become `NOT NULL` and unique ignoring case. In SQLite that
+requires rebuilding the table and creating a `NOCASE` unique index. The
+production snapshot (`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`, owned by Support) violates
+both requirements, and the migration is not allowed to modify or delete
+customer rows, so it cannot complete.
+
+## Conflicting rows
+
+- **NULL email** — `id = 17` (`Goran Okafor`). A `NOT NULL` column cannot
+  receive this row during the rebuild. No value can be invented for it without
+  editing customer data.
+- **Case-insensitive duplicates** — `ana.silva@example.com` appears at
+  `id = 1` and `id = 23` (identical) and at `id = 4` as
+  `Ana.Silva@example.com` (differs only in case). A unique index on
+  `email COLLATE NOCASE` rejects all three.
+
+Reproduce:
+
+```sql
+SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE email IS NULL;
+-- 17|Goran Okafor
+
+SELECT email COLLATE NOCASE, group_concat(id)
+FROM users WHERE email IS NOT NULL
+GROUP BY email COLLATE NOCASE HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;
+-- ana.silva@example.com|1,4,23
+```
+
+Any migration that succeeds against the snapshot would have to delete rows
+1/4/23, blank or invent an email for row 17, or skip the actual constraint
+(e.g. enforcing it only with triggers, leaving the existing violations in
+place). All of those are workarounds the task rules out: the data is Support's
+to fix, and a constraint that the stored data violates does not satisfy the
+requirement.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` contains the real constraint work:
+`email TEXT NOT NULL COLLATE NOCASE UNIQUE`, applied via a table rebuild. It
+begins with a precheck that aborts with
+`users_email_must_be_non_null_and_case_insensitively_unique` if any row is NULL
+or case-insensitively duplicated, so deploying it fails loudly and atomically
+rather than partially or after guessing at data. On a fresh database the
+migration applies and both constraints are enforced.
+
+The fixture and the tests were not modified.
+
+## What Support needs to do
+
+1. Give every user an email (`id = 17` currently has none).
+2. Resolve the `ana.silva@example.com` cluster (ids 1, 4, 23), keeping the
+   address of the account that should own it and moving the others to distinct
+   addresses.
+3. Re-run this migration once the checks above return no rows; it will then
+   rebuild the table without touching row contents.
+
+Migration status: fresh DB verified; production snapshot blocked.
